#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:343
msgid "4. An array is an object (advanced)"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:345
msgid "In JavaScript, arrays are implemented as **objects**:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:355
msgid "The indexes are the keys `\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, `\"2\"` of that object. Because of this, a wrong \"index\" gives **no error** - it simply becomes an ordinary **property** of the object, and it is not counted in `length`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:370
msgid "Arrays also come with built-in properties and **methods** (functions attached to the array) for the common operations: `length`, `push`, `pop`, `slice` and many more. You do not need to learn them by heart - they are well documented in [MDN: Array](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array). Today you meet the ones rules use most; the rest (`map`, `filter`, `sort`, ...) come in session 7."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:377
msgid "Is it an array? `Array.isArray`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:379
msgid "JSON data does not always have the shape you expect. A list can be missing, can be `null`, or can be one object instead of a list with one object. `typeof` cannot tell an array from an object - `Array.isArray` can:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:390
msgid "References: two names, one array"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:392
msgid "A variable holds a **reference** to the array, not the array itself. Copying the variable copies the reference - both names point to the **same** array:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:404
msgid "`===` compares arrays **by reference**: \"is it the same array?\", not \"are the elements the same?\"."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:406
msgid "`const` protects the **variable** (the arrow), not the array: `fruits.push(...)` and `fruits[0] = ...` are allowed, `fruits = [...]` is an error."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:416
msgid "A **copy** is a new array with the same elements: `arr.slice()` or `[...arr]` (section 5)."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:426
msgid "Do not change the report"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:427
msgid "In a rule, `doc` belongs to the engine. If one part of a rule changes a list of `doc` (with `push`, `splice`, `sort`, `length = 0` ...), every later line sees the changed data. Work on a copy, or build a new array."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:432
msgid "Comparing two arrays"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:433
msgid "To check if two short arrays of strings or numbers have the same elements, compare their text: `a.join(\",\") === b.join(\",\")` (section 7). The homework checks in this course do the same with `JSON.stringify` (session 9)."
msgstr ""

