msgid "`if (!enclosures || !enclosures.length) return;` and `if (!Array.isArray(enclosures)) return;` - do they behave the same for `[]`? For `undefined`? For one object `{ id: \"ENC-01\", ... }`?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:1724
msgid "A report has `firmware_level: \"4.2.1.7\"` - with no `(build ...)` part. What is `version` in both rules?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:1728
msgid "Style only: `var` -> `const` / `let`; `new Array(...)` -> `[...]`; `indexOf(model) == -1` -> `!includes(model)` (the same for strings); `==` / `!=` -> `===` / `!==`; the `for` with an index -> `for...of`; `== true` -> nothing; `+` -> template strings; the `&&` chain -> `?.`; `new Object()` + `details[\"...\"]` -> an object literal. Could change the result: the guard for `enclosures` (questions 3 and 4) and `String(... || \"\")` vs `?? \"\"`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:1735
msgid "An empty array of length 2 (two holes), not `[2]`. `[...]` always means \"these elements\"."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:1737
msgid "A string has `length` (6), so the old guard lets it through, and the loop walks the **characters**: `enc` is `\"E\"`, `\"N\"`, ... `enc.state` and `enc.firmware` are `undefined`, so every character gives two \"problems\" (`versionBelow(undefined, \"2.14\")` is true too: `parseInt` gives `NaN`, and `|| 0` makes it 0). Twelve problems like `\"undefined is undefined\"` - a nonsense alert. The modern rule stops: a string is not an array."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:1743
msgid "`[]`: the old rule stops at the guard (`![].length` is true); the modern one loops zero times and stops at `problems.length === 0` - the same result. `undefined`: both stop. One object: the object has no `length`, so the old rule stops; `Array.isArray` is false, so the modern rule stops too - both **miss** the enclosure. Fix: `const enclosures = asList(doc.inventory.enclosures);` (section 10)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-arrays-and-records.md:1749
msgid "`\"4.2.1.7\"` in both: when there is no space, `split(\" \")` gives an array with the whole string, and `[0]` is that string."
msgstr ""

